  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front is slowly making its way eastward\nacross NC this hour per satellite and surface observation\nanalysis, forecast to stall along the coast as overall forcing\nweakens and shifts away from the region. Starting to see some\ngradual aggravation of cu fields along the immediate coast with\nenhanced convergence ahead of the frontal boundary and along\ndiurnal sound and sea breezes. Some additional lift is being\nprovided by a weak shortwave pivoting around deep mid-level\ntroughing swirling over the eastern CONUS.\n\nPre-storm environment today appears marginally favorable for\nstrong to severe storms with MLCAPEs climbing to 2500-3000 J/kg\nand deep layer shear, especially in the vicinity of the boundary\nlayer, of around 30-35 kt. Multi-cell clusters are the favored\nstorm mode today but a few weak supercells are not out of the\nquestion. Healthy amount of dry air aloft and forecast DCAPEs of\n1000+ J/kg point to damaging winds once again being the main\nthreat, but in supercells some rotating updrafts could support\nsome hail and a low risk of a tornado. The convective risk \nshould primarily be diurnally-driven, but some overnight\nredevelopment of showers and storms is possible as the front\nwill remain in the area, and elevated instability will remain\nabundant. Overall moisture is not as deep as the past few days\nand the overall flooding threat is comparatively lower.\n\nGuidance continues to depict a weak surface low lifting NE along\nthe frontal boundary tomorrow (the low may actually subtly pull\nthe boundary farther inland as it progresses). Overall pre-storm\nenvironment will be similar to tomorrow, sans a modest uptick in\neffective shear in the vicinity of the low. This may provide a\nmarginally better environment for supercell development. If the\nfront does get pulled west, the convective risk will also follow\nsuit. The greatest risk of strong to severe thunderstorms\nremains east of HWY 17 with damaging winds remaining the primary\nthreat, with hail possible in supercells.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance still struggling on whether or not the\nairmass can fully dry out Tuesday and Wednesday as our current\nfrontal boundary may still be stalled nearby. Isolated showers\nand storms cannot be ruled out but coverage is likely to be far\nsparser than the past few days. Moisture and instability then \nbegin to rebuild across the Southeast U.S. coastline with the \npotential for another round of unsettled weather.",
